question:
A single glucagon molecule activates one receptor on a liver cell, triggering the following sequence of events:

- the activated receptor switches on 50 molecules of the G-protein Gs;
- each active Gs switches on one molecule of adenylyl cyclase, and each adenylyl cyclase catalyses the formation of 200 molecules of cyclic AMP;
- four molecules of cyclic AMP are required to activate one molecule of protein kinase A (PKA);
- each active PKA molecule then phosphorylates 100 molecules of phosphorylase kinase.

Assuming that every cyclic AMP molecule produced is used to activate PKA, how many molecules of phosphorylase kinase are activated as a result of one glucagon molecule?

A hormone binds a G-protein-coupled receptor on a smooth-muscle cell and causes a rapid rise in cytosolic Ca2+ concentration. Investigators find that this Ca2+ rise:

- still occurs when the cells are bathed in a Ca2+-free extracellular solution;
- is abolished by a specific inhibitor of phospholipase C;
- is NOT affected by an inhibitor of adenylyl cyclase.

Which of the following best explains these observations?
